|term[sequence-cause]
  :status settled
  :subject-field addressing
  :layer framing-vocabulary
  :gloss Why order appears in an address at all: three causes, per-segment, and exactly what an engine may optimize.
  |designation sequence-cause
    :status preferred
    :channel prose
  |definition
    :defined-by @claim-sequence-causes
    :anchor sequence-causes
    :projection paraphrase
    A sequence of components appears in an address for three different reasons — subset-sequence (nested conjunction wearing a path costume), resolver-sequence (truly imperative), compositional (foldable iff endpoint-only and associative) — and the imperative-vs-declarative character of a segment is a derived property of its cause.
  |formal
    :notation <latex:cause(s_i) \in \{ \supseteq, \to, \circ \}>
    :reading Subset-sequences reorder and fuse; compositional sequences fold when endpoint-only; resolver-sequences are pipeline barriers.
  |rel :kind property-of :to @path
  |invariant
    Exhaustiveness is honestly open: three causes are demonstrated; "exactly three" is not argued (candidate fourth: mid-address perspective-shift, pending the act-anatomy adjudication).
